    <briefdescription>
<para>GPU training loop coordinator. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Orchestrates the training lifecycle for GPU-backed ML workloads:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Mini-batch iteration</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Forward / backward pass via a caller-supplied <computeroutput><ref refid="classthemis_1_1gpu_1_1_g_p_u_training_loop_1acc89f331cd89dbda5431f9c84c96a1e5" kindref="member">LossFn</ref></computeroutput></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Loss tracking, step counting, early stopping</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Periodic checkpoint callbacks</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Per-epoch statistics</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>Like <computeroutput><ref refid="classthemis_1_1gpu_1_1_g_p_u_launcher" kindref="compound">GPULauncher</ref></computeroutput>, the actual GPU computation is supplied as a <computeroutput><ref refid="classthemis_1_1gpu_1_1_g_p_u_training_loop_1acc89f331cd89dbda5431f9c84c96a1e5" kindref="member">LossFn</ref></computeroutput> callback so the coordinator is testable without real hardware. When THEMIS_ENABLE_CUDA / THEMIS_ENABLE_HIP are defined the <computeroutput><ref refid="classthemis_1_1gpu_1_1_g_p_u_training_loop_1acc89f331cd89dbda5431f9c84c96a1e5" kindref="member">LossFn</ref></computeroutput> body would invoke a CUDA/ROCm kernel and return the device-side loss value.</para>
<para>Thread safety: public methods are protected by an internal mutex. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
